 \begin{abstract}
-Detailed balance removes directional cycle flow from a Markov model, potentially reducing estimation variance at the cost of restriction bias. We evaluate this trade-off for next-session open-to-close return terciles in SPY, QQQ and IWM, using 2,766 forecasts per asset over 2015--2025. An expanding-window design compares IID, unrestricted three-state Markov and reversible Markov forecasts under common information sets and a fixed intraday allocation rule. The unrestricted model has the lowest log loss and Brier score for each asset. Across six log-loss contrasts, Romano--Wolf adjusted $p$-values for unrestricted versus IID are 0.093, 0.008 and 0.048, respectively; the IWM result is sensitive to the adjustment method. No reversible-minus-unrestricted log-loss interval excludes zero. A score decomposition places most SPY and QQQ improvement in middle-versus-outer tercile prediction, to which the allocation is locally less sensitive; IWM also improves conditional upper-versus-lower prediction. Mean net gains over IID are only 0.043, 0.020 and 0.073 basis points per day. With exposure near one-half and daily entry and exit, all nine model allocations lose more than 64\% cumulatively at five basis points per side. Alternative estimation rules preserve the full-period ranking, but calendar segments reveal substantial instability. These results provide no demonstrated forecast benefit from detailed balance in this design. They distinguish modest probability-score gains from profitability under a particular decision rule, without establishing model equivalence or ruling out economic value under other rules.
+Detailed balance removes directional cycle flow from a Markov model, trading estimation variance against restriction bias. We evaluate that trade-off for next-session open-to-close return terciles in SPY, QQQ and IWM, using 2,766 forecasts per asset over 2015--2025, common information sets and a fixed intraday rule. The unrestricted model has the lowest log loss and Brier score for each asset, but the evidence is conditional. Across six log-loss contrasts, Romano--Wolf adjusted $p$-values for unrestricted versus IID are 0.093, 0.008 and 0.048; the IWM result is sensitive to the adjustment method and SPY is not significant. No reversible-minus-unrestricted interval excludes zero. A score decomposition places most SPY and QQQ improvement in middle-versus-outer terciles, to which the allocation is locally less sensitive; IWM also improves conditional upper-versus-lower prediction. Mean net gains over IID are 0.043, 0.020 and 0.073 basis points per day, against about five basis points of round-trip cost, so acting costs roughly a hundred times the difference it exploits and the rule's losses follow from arithmetic rather than from model ranking. The advantage is concentrated before 2020 and reverses afterwards. No forecast benefit from detailed balance is demonstrated, and the unrestricted advantage is a conditional finding rather than a stable edge.
 \end{abstract}
 
 \noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Markov chains; detailed balance; probabilistic forecasting; intraday returns; transaction costs; time reversibility.
